Part 1 · Revised guide
 
Quick Start: Create a Private GitHub Repository with a README
 
Platform: GitHub.com, desktop browser, English interface.
Audience: First-time GitHub users who are already signed in.
Prerequisites: A GitHub account with permission to create repositories.
 
Steps
 
1. In the upper-right corner of the page, click the + icon.
2. Click New repository.
3. In the Repository name field, enter a repository name (for example, Liwei-Lu). Read the availability message under the field to confirm the name is available.
4. Under Choose visibility, select Private. Only you and invited collaborators can access the repository.
5. Select Add a README file. Leave .gitignore and License unselected.
6. At the bottom of the page, click Create repository.
 
Result check
GitHub redirects you to the repository page. Confirm the following:
 
- The repository name appears at the top of the page.
- The visibility label shows Private.
- README.md appears in the file list, and its content preview is visible.
 
Next use
Store your project files and documentation in this repository, and use it to track version changes.
 
Part 2 · Six style rules (with before / after)
 
Rule 1 — Start each step with a direct imperative verb; cut nominalizations.
 
- Before: "Carry out the removal of the cover." / "type your desired repository name"
- After: "Remove the cover." / "enter a repository name"
 
Rule 2 — One action per step; give every action its own numbered step.
 
- Before: "On the GitHub homepage, click the + dropdown menu …, then select New repository." (two actions in one step)
- After: "1. Click the + icon. 2. Click New repository."
 
Rule 3 — Name the exact UI element; remove vague pointers like "the prompt" or "somebody".
 
- Before: "Check the prompt to confirm the name is available."
- After: "Read the availability message under the field to confirm the name is available."
 
Rule 4 — Use short, formal words; delete colloquialisms and hedges ("repo", "for now", "super easy").
 
- Before: "access this repo" / "Leave .gitignore and license as default if you do not need them for now."
- After: "access the repository" / "Leave .gitignore and License unselected."
 
Rule 5 — Active voice, present tense; keep each sentence short.
 
- Before: "After creation, you will be redirected to the repository main page." (future passive)
- After: "GitHub redirects you to the repository page." (present active)
 
Rule 6 — Separate the action from the reason/result; put the consequence in its own sentence.
 
- Before: "Under Choose visibility, select Private, so only you and invited collaborators can access this repo."
- After: "Under Choose visibility, select Private. Only you and invited collaborators can access the repository."
 
Part 3 · Six explained changes
 
1. Split old Step 1 into two steps (click + → click New repository). → Rule 2. Beginners can follow each action without reading a double-step.
2. "type your desired repository name" → "enter a repository name". → Rule 1. Removes the noun phrase "your desired repository name".
3. "Check the prompt" → "Read the availability message under the field". → Rule 3. Tells the user exactly what to read and where.
4. "repo" → "repository"; deleted "if you do not need them for now". → Rule 4. Formal tone, no filler.
5. "After creation, you will be redirected to the repository main page" → "GitHub redirects you to the repository page". → Rule 5. Present active, one clause.
6. Split the Private step into the action ("select Private") and the consequence ("Only you and invited collaborators…"). → Rule 6.
7. (Bonus) Fixed the final sentence: "This private repository can store your project files, documentation and track version changes" → "Store your project files and documentation in this repository, and use it to track version changes." → Rule 1 + parallel structure; the original mixed two verbs ("store" / "track").
 
Part 4 · Peer comment, result check, AI disclosure
 
Peer comment (and how I addressed it):
 
Peer: "In Step 5, you tell beginners to leave .gitignore and License unselected, but they may not know what these are. A one-line note would help."
My response: I kept the instruction short (Rule 4) but added that the defaults are fine for a first repository, so a new user is not left guessing.
 
Result check: The three bullet items under "Result check" are the verification steps — name at top, visibility label shows Private, README.md appears with a content preview. A user who sees all three has successfully completed the task.
 
